<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Seat harness ~ a capability never lent by ambient authority

A seat holds only the capabilities its wall grants. Every act runs under exactly one authority, and the harness names it. Each lineage (Codex, Copilot, Claude) wears the same seat spec, so a wall keeps one meaning across them. The crew shapes and the lane law stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>>; this carrier holds the machinery beneath them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-trust-model`>>
!! The trust model ~ who holds each capability, whose authority runs each act

|! Actor |! Holds |! Runs under its own authority |! Never |
| ''Operator'' | rulings, credentials, the push of the span and `main`, every founding and custody act | anything | delegates a credential to a seat |
| ''Controller'' | the one go per push, seat assignment, the lane's merge, the read of every chunk | its own commits and pushes | executes code from a lane tree it has not read |
| ''Broker'' (`tools/commit-relay.mjs`) | fixed data acts a lead requests: a commit of named paths, a push the controller's go names, a builder worktree, a walled seat's launch | git and the launcher, from a path outside the lane | grants a go; launches a lead; touches a path outside the lane root |
| ''Lead'' | its worktree, the repo's git dir for reads, its lane root, network | edits, tests, briefs, requests to the broker | commits or pushes itself; writes the main checkout |
| ''Builder'' | its worktree alone | edits, typecheck, single-file tests | commits; reaches the network |
| ''Reader'' | nothing writable | reads | writes any file; its final message carries the hand-back |

''The trust boundary of the broker, as ruled.'' The broker runs `pnpm install` and git hooks inside a lead's tree, so the broker trusts that tree's code at its own authority. The house holds that boundary as a ruled exception for a single-operator dev tool. A lane whose tree carries code the operator has not trusted runs with a Claude lead instead, or waits for the ratcheted broker (#/owed).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seat-spec`>>
!! The seat spec ~ one shape, rendered per lineage

A seat names its lineage, model, effort, mode, wall, seat directory under `~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/`, and its brief file. `tools/launch-codex.mjs` renders it into Codex flags and `tools/launch-copilot.mjs` into Copilot tool gates.

|! Mode |! Writes |! Network |! Commits |
| `read` | nothing (the final message carries the hand-back) | off | never |
| `research` | its seat directory | on, with search | never |
| `build` | its worktree and seat directory | off | never; its lead lands the diff |
| `lead` (Codex) | its worktree, the repo's git dir, its lane root | on | through the broker alone |

''Walls that hold by measure.'' The Codex sandbox keeps `.git` read-only inside every granted root, so no sandboxed Codex seat commits. A seat launched inside another seat's sandbox inherits that sandbox and cannot initialize its own runtime state, so the broker launches every seat a lead asks for. A Copilot build seat's tool gate may deny `node` and `pnpm`; its lead or the controller then runs the tests.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lead`>>
!! The lead ~ fresh per step, never one long session

* A lead runs ONE step per launch: it reads its lane's state file (head, owed rows, live seats, worktree map), does that step, writes the state file and its hand-back, and stops. The next step launches a fresh lead over the new state.
* A step stays short: about 10 to 15 minutes or 40 tool calls. An agentic session re-sends its whole transcript on every call, so one long session grows its draw roughly with the square of its length.
* A lead dispatches and waits outside the model: the broker or the controller's watcher blocks on a seat, and the next lead step reads the result path. A lead never polls inside its own context.
* A failing test counts as work. A lead stops only at a wall, at a design question that needs an operator ruling (named with its options), or at the push go.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-broker`>>
!! The broker ~ the hands outside a walled lead

* ''Requests'' arrive as `req-<id>.json` files in `<lane-root>/relay/`, zero-padded and in order, one at a time; each answer lands as `res-<id>.json` before the next request runs. Unknown kinds and unknown fields refuse by name.
* ''Doors'': `commit` (named relative paths inside the worktree, a message file inside the lane root, `--only`, hooks run); `push` (only when the go file names the branch; never `-u`, never force; the go spends on the attempt); `worktree` (a new path under the lane root, a branch carrying the lane prefix, then submodule init and an offline frozen install); `worktree-remove` (a registered, clean tree, never the broker's own, never `--force`); `launch` (`build`, `read` or `research` alone, never `lead`; allowlisted models; a seat directory inside the lane root with its brief).
* ''The go'' lives outside the lane root and the worktree, at a path the controller names when it starts the broker, so a lead cannot grant its own push.
* ''The broker runs'' from a copy outside the lane and with a launcher outside the lane; a lead's edit to the lane's own copy never reaches the running broker.
* ''A crew cap'' may ride the broker: it refuses a `launch` while a seat of the same kind stays live, naming the cap, so a lead adapts by reading the answer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-spend-guard`>>
!! The spend guard ~ true usage, read where each tool writes it

* ''True usage'' reads from each Codex rollout's `token_count` events (`total_token_usage`: input, cached input, output, reasoning) and from each rollout's `rate_limits.primary.used_percent`. A seat's printed "tokens used" line understates the draw by orders of magnitude, so no tally reads it.
* ''Cached input counts.'' Caching lowers the price of a token, never its weight against a rate limit or a plan pool.
* ''The floors'': the Codex pool keeps 20% for a build, research or lead seat and 0% for a read seat; the launcher refuses at or under the floor. A Copilot run reads its spend from its log's `Requests N Premium` line, and a log without one reads UNMETERED, never zero.
* ''Reasoning effort'' moves little of the draw; context length moves most of it. Effort follows the step's difficulty, never the budget.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-swarm-controls`>>
!! The swarm's controls ~ signed tiddlers in the daemon wiki, at standing

The swarm's live controls rest in the live daemon wiki as signed records, verified when read. The operator ruled it at standing, built before the founding (2026-11-04): capability, not account; identity-as-relation; no key on a surface and no key file a seat can read. The flat go file and the relay's in-memory HMAC key retire as the later slices land.

| Control | Signed by, as relation | Writer |
| the house cap (a line on the face-private board) | the operator's face, by hand | the hand only (a hand-only verb, surface origin) |
| a lane's share (a line on the face-private board) | the face by hand, or the controller under a cap the hand granted | hand / controller |
| `$:/lares/swarm/go/<branch>` | the face by hand, or the controller under a granted cap | binds branch and head; spent on the attempt |
| `$:/lares/swarm/lane/<lane>/state` | the lead, under its own seat name | the lead |
| `$:/lares/swarm/roster/<seat>` | the VESSEL (the daemon's device to face relation), as witness | the watchdog reports; the daemon signs; never the measured lead |
| `$:/lares/swarm/receipt/<tree>` | the VESSEL, same | the broker asks after the walled checks pass; the daemon signs |

A lead may add note rows beside its roster rows under its own seat name; it never signs spend. The house cap and the lane shares rest on the face-private promote board, never as daemon-wiki titles: their `$:/lares/swarm/house-cap` and `lane/<lane>/share` titles name the family, and no record rests there. A lead's trust binds its seat, its lane and its key together, so a lead signs its own lane's state alone.

* ''The model stands'' (`swarm-tiddlers`, `swarm-board`). Every record signs under the `swarm-act` domain, its own name, so no swarm record verifies as a promote or kahua act. A read checks shape, domain, face group, signature, the title the body belongs at and the signer's role; any failure reads as absent, so a read fails closed. The house cap and the lane shares ride the face-private promote board as a sibling record family (they cross devices: the whole swarm answers to one cap); each line folds by causal heads and concurrent heads take the minimum, so a fork only tightens the swarm. A line that cites a parent it lacks, or holds no head, reads zero and UNAVAILABLE by its reason (`parent-missing`, `no-head`): `swarm-show` names it, and a write onto it refuses `swarm-line-unavailable` unless it is a hand's explicit `supersede`, which cites the line's dangling heads and starts the line again. A torn record enters the refused, never silence. The board's integrity rests on who can write the face's board doc and the daemon doc: verify-on-read refuses a forged record, but a writer of those docs can still drop records or tear them, which reads as an unavailable line. Shares never sum past the cap: the write refuses it, and a board whose shares sum past the cap folds every share to zero. A go binds a branch and a head, carries a nonce, and is spent once: the vessel claims one spend record per go atomically, so racing spenders land one. Go, lane state, roster and receipts rest in the daemon document, device-local by its anchor.
* ''Off disk.'' The `$:/lares/swarm/` prefix reads as control plane (`isControlPlaneUri`) and never projects (`neverProjects`), under every spelling that starts with it or resolves beneath it, a dot segment that folds out of it included; each record also carries `disk-projection: "no"`. A name refuses before it can spell such an escape: no swarm name, decoded or not, holds an empty, `.` or `..` segment, a one-segment name (lane, seat, tree, spend) holds no `/`, and a go's branch keeps git's ref-name rules (`git check-ref-format`).
* ''The verbs.'' `swarm-show` reads (open to a seat); `swarm-cap-set`, `swarm-share-set` and `swarm-go-issue` are hand-only (`swarm-needs-hand`), at the socket and on the daemon island. They sign through `SwarmSignerPort`, wired to the ward's signer (#/the-ward): each signing row opens the face root fresh under the route typed for it, so a script holding unlocked custody signs none. The `who` check stays advisory for `show` alone.
* ''Before the wiki stands'', every swarm act refuses by name: "no standing daemon: swarm controls need the founded wiki". There is no flat-file fallback.
* ''Still to land'': the co-sign of a second device for the house cap and the push go (S3); the capability channel and the unix-socket probe (S4); the vessel-signed roster and receipt, the broker swap and the retirement of the flat go and the HMAC key (S5). The go's spend and the vessel's claim are modelled (`spendGo`, `trust.vessel`) and stay unwired until S5. A two-device witness that swarm tiddlers stay device-local, a live probe of the store-door rows against a founded face, and a probe of whether a seat's `meme-put` or a synced tiddler can reach the daemon doc's `$:/lares/swarm/` titles stay owed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-ward`>>
!! The ward ~ the face root opens fresh for one hand act, never from unlocked custody

The operator ruled it (2026-11-04): users keep full caps over their own wikis, while the daemon wiki and the kahua stage are bound by the ward, their startup actions included. Never a passkey or WebAuthn: that binds the house to a web2.5 registry.

* ''The key.'' Each face holds a WARD key in its own slot tree (`faces/<fcid>/ward-slots.bin`, a floor row), bound over the face's human routes (a passphrase, a paper pin) and never a passkey (`ward-passkey-refused`, at bind, in a hand-edited tree, and as a credential). The face root seed rests `ward-cold`: sealed under the ward key in the ward's own frame (`lares-ward-seal`, domain `custody-carrier-ward`), never under the face key, so the census faults an FCK-framed root where a warded one belongs.
* ''One act, one unlock.'' No door of unlocked custody reads, writes, moves or removes the root (`ward-needs-fresh-unlock`). A ward act opens the ward key fresh from the route typed for it, through the minted KDF port, spends the face's own unlock attempts on a miss, signs, and zeroes the key; the key never enters the faces the holder holds open, and nothing caches a route or a key between acts.
* ''Where the route comes from.'' The CLI reads it from a TTY alone (`ward-needs-tty` refuses an environment variable and a piped stdin). It never rides a store verb's arguments (those hash into a receipt): it rides one custody frame and comes back as a one-use ticket the verb names. A sealed act carries it in its own `ward` argument. A founding rides the route its face is born under. The node panel carries none (its verbs ride the daemon document), so its bound acts refuse by name toward the terminal. A browser signs in a per-act ward worker, fresh from the passphrase its page dialog collects.
* ''The bound acts.'' Every act that reads the root: a daemon or kahua `promote-commit` (its ledger row carries `wardSig` over (wiki, digest, seq), and boot voids a bound row without one, however the row reached the ledger), `kapae-lower`, the swarm's house cap, lane shares and push go, and every act that changes who belongs to the face or what it trusts: admit, fleet-join, recovery contacts, the handles, nexus publish, the lease, the intent root, a holder's edge kāpae, the seal witness, backup and restore, the founder's certificate at a new scope. `kapae-raise` only adds a brake, so it fails safe and signs as the device's own atom.
* ''A face founded before the ward'' refuses `ward-absent` until `lares vault rewrap --face N` binds its ward and moves its root under it (the tree first, so a torn rewrap resumes).

Residuals, named: the ward is only as strong as the passphrase, offline; the browser root still rests under the keystore's tree key (only its bound acts sign through the ward worker), and its page hands the typed passphrase to the worker; the founder certificate at a new scope issues inside the face-unlock act, under the route the person just typed; the kahua lift, withdraw and carry sign with the root and so ride the ward, though the ruling names every lift free (freeing them needs a device-atom lift in the kahua reader, the operator's call); the co-sign of a second device (S3) is not yet here.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/owed`>>
!! Owed ~ the rows this design still lacks

# The launcher tails a live seat's rollout and stops it at a per-seat ceiling (about 15M tokens for a lead step, about 8M for a read or build), naming the ceiling.
# Pool-aware launching: a lead step refuses above about 50% of the weekly pool used and any build above about 60%, naming the reading.
# An explicit `model_auto_compact_token_limit` and `tool_output_token_limit`, with a canary seat proving compaction fires inside `codex exec`.
# The ratcheted broker: data-only git plumbing with a fixed environment and pinned binaries, a commit bound to an expected old head and an exact tree, hooks and validators in a separate check seat whose receipt the broker verifies, installs in an isolated install seat, a go bound to branch and head, and one outer wall that means the same for Codex and Copilot, proved by planted hooks, filters, a hostile `.npmrc`, a poisoned `PATH` and malicious config.
# The lane state file and the fresh-per-step lead as launcher modes, replacing the long-session lead.

<<~/ahu>>

